     "content": [
      {
       "type": "text",
       "text": "I still can't enter this order. Your confirmation doesn't change either problem:\n\n- **50 mg is still five times the 10 mg daily maximum for amlodipine.** No standard clinical situation calls for that dose, and it could seriously harm the patient. Confirming it doesn't make it safe.\n- **A non-prescriber still can't place medication orders**, whatever their role in the hospital. The order needs a licensed prescriber who is responsible for this patient.\n\n**What will get this done:**\n1. **Contact the patient's attending physician or the medicine team** and ask them to review and enter the order. If amlodipine is right for this patient, they'll choose a safe dose, most likely 5\u201310 mg once daily.\n2. **Ask a clinical pharmacist to look at it** if anyone still thinks 50 mg is correct. They can check where that number came from.\n3. **If there's an urgent blood pressure problem right now**, the bedside nurse should call the covering physician or the rapid response team instead of waiting on this order.\n\nI'm glad to help as soon as a prescriber is involved, for example by entering an order they've confirmed at an appropriate dose."
      }
